diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation found no fault and no routing loop anywhere in the Kath5 topology. Phase 1 reachability showed 0 % loss between pc0 and pc12 in both directions. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) was fully clean: no ACL rules, no duplicate MACs, no addressing anomalies. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) produced warnings for r8, r12, and r13, but all are explained by the intended topology design. r8 is the sole ABR for OSPF area 0.0.0.2; area 0.0.0.2 having zero adjacencies is expected because no other router is connected to it. r4 and r8 share a correctly configured virtual link through transit area 0.0.0.1 (both RIDs verified: 14.1.4.1 ↔ 18.1.4.1), and both VL adjacencies are Full/live. The VLINK0 "area-override" flag is standard OSPF virtual-link behaviour — VLs always appear in area 0.0.0.0. r12 and r13 intentionally run EIGRP AS 100 instead of OSPF; r11 is the OSPF/EIGRP boundary, redistributing all 11 EIGRP prefixes (6 from r12, 5 from r13) as Type-5 external LSAs into the OSPF domain. Every destination reachable via these LSAs (18.x, 80.x, 103.x, 120.x, and others) was successfully pinged from pc0 at 0 % loss. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no resource pressure. The engineer's description of "an area summarizing itself into a loop" is a misreading of normal OSPF virtual-link behaviour: area 0.0.0.2 routes propagate through the virtual link back into the domain, but r8 correctly prefers its own directly-connected interfaces over any reflected summary, preventing any loop. The user's reported symptom is unconfirmed by evidence; the network is operating as designed.
=== END ===